    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The National Basketball Association imposes two important, and unusual, restrictions on\r\nits new employees: a draft, in which they are assigned a single employer with which they\r\ncan contract with, and a pay-scale, which prescribes maximum compensation levels for\r\nup to the first four years of their careers. Using a sample of veteran players, who have\r\ngone through free-agency, I establish the value owners ascribe to a player-season in the\r\nopen free-agent market. Comparing this value to player compensation, under rookie\r\ncontracts, allows me to examine the surplus generated by new players for their teams. I\r\nfind that the two rookie restrictions allow owners to extract significant monopsony rents\r\nfrom new players; the rents are highest for top draft choices, and decline steeply as the\r\ndraft goes on, implying that owners are rational surplus-maximizers.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
